  "query_preview": "..(Friday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 453 PM EDT WED SEP 30 2026\n\nThe models are in good agreement with more of a split-flow long\nwave pattern in place across the CONUS at the end of the work \nweek. A progressive short wave trough will start out over the \nUpper Midwest/Great Lakes on Friday within the northern stream. \nMeanwhile, a positively tilted southern stream short wave trough \nwill be positioned from the middle Mississippi Valley down through\nnorthern Mexico. Ridging will be centered off of the southeastern\nU.S. coast, while another high is sprawled from portions of the \nFour Corners region to just offshore of the Southern California \ncoast. Through the weekend, the southern stream positively tilted \ntrough will gradually shift east with time. This feature will then\neventually be absorbed into amplifying northern stream energy by \nearly Monday. The northern stream will then hold sway generally \neast of the Rockies through the middle of next week, with ridging \nremaining anchored across the West. All of this will result in \nunsettled weather across eastern Kentucky into the weekend, with \ncooler and drier weather to follow for the first half of next \nweek. \n\nRain chances will be on the increase from northwest to southeast \nacross eastern Kentucky on Friday, as well above normal PWATs\n(95th percentile) surge in ahead of an approaching surface cold\nfront. Fortunately, two things are working against the threat of \nexcessive runoff across the area. The first is antecedent dry \nconditions heading into this event, with most waterways running\nfairly low. The other will be the instability, as both LREF and\nNBM probabilities for greater amounts (>500 J/kg) of SB CAPE and \nMU CAPE have generally been trending down across our area. As \nsuch, without better thunder potential, the majority of the \nhigher PWAT air will likely remain untapped. This is also seen in \nthe raw QPF numbers, with probabilities for seeing greater than 1 \ninch of rainfall in 24 hours ending 8 am Saturday across the area \nremaining pretty meager from the NBM (10% southeast and 30-40% \nnorthwest). \n\nAs the cold front exits our area Friday evening, widespread \nshower activity will gradually diminish from northwest to \nsoutheast. For Saturday, the better moisture gradient has shifted \na bit further into Tennessee this go around, with a larger PoP \ngradient noted in the blended guidance from northwest to southeast.\nMostly cloudy skies will still be in place, with lingering low \nlevel northeasterly flow in the wake of the cold front. A \nsecondary surge of moisture will move in for Saturday night and \nSunday, with higher PoPs favored across our terrain counties. \nWhile a stray thunderstorm can not be completely ruled out, this \nis also looking less and less likely, given the weaker \ninstability.\n\nCooler progressive surface high pressure will follow Sunday night,\nmoving in from the Plains. This high will shift east with time,\nsettling across the Ohio Valley by Tuesday, before positioning \nover New England by Wednesday. This will result in dry weather \nacross eastern Kentucky after Sunday. Cooler northeasterly flow \nwill remain in place through Tuesday, with below normal \ntemperatures expected. Highs will average in the 60s and 70s, \nwith overnight lows in the 40s. Some modest return flow ensues by \nWednesday, with temperatures returning closer to normal values.",
